#{[17592186363807 :question/problem "A communications beacon of mass $24\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the beacon's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\n\nThe air resistance on the beacon is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the beacon’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the beacon are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $\\mathbf{t}$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363810 :question/problem "A sampling canister of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the canister is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the canister’s velocity is $9\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the canister are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $\\mathbf{t}$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186373003 :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies $y(2)=-1.$\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"hidden\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"hidden\"></td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\" data-border-left=\"hidden\">$y(x)=$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"bottom\" data-border-top=\"hidden\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"hidden\">$x$</td> </tr> <tr> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\" data-border-left=\"hidden\"> </td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\"> </td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-right=\"hidden\" data-border-bottom=\"hidden\" data-border-left=\"hidden\">{{field-1}} </td> </tr> </tbody></table></div>"] [17592186373785 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}-2})={\\color{blue}-1}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1} & =\\frac{A}{({\\color{red}-2})} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$"] [17592186377695 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$"] [17592186377695 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"] [17592186377708 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$"] [17592186377708 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186378622 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y.$"] [17592186378622 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378629 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$"] [17592186378629 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"] [17592186378636 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186378636 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186380041 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$"] [17592186380041 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380048 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$"] [17592186380048 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380055 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$"] [17592186380055 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"] [17592186380242 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186380242 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [17592186381209 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x)-{y}^{2}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =-{y}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x-2x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =-(1)^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-\\pi  \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381216 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({3}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)-{3}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({3}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381218 :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}"] [17592186381220 :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] [17592186381221 :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] [17592186381244 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\cdot (x\\cdot 0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186381247 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}"] [17592186381248 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}"]}
